['```
#include <iostream>
#include <forward_list>
#include <string>

void insertAfter(std::forward_list<std::string>& list, const std::string& target, const std::string& newStr) {
    // Search for the first occurrence of the target string in the forward list
    auto iter = list.begin();
    while (iter != list.end() && *iter != target) {
        ++iter;
    }

    // If the target string is not found, append the new string to the end of the list
    if (iter == list.end()) {
        list.push_back(newStr);
    } else {
        // Insert the new string immediately following the target string
        iter = list.insert_after(iter, newStr);
    }
}

int main() {
    std::forward_list<std::string> list = {"apple", "banana", "cherry"};
    insertAfter(list, "banana", "durian");
    for (const auto& str : list) {
        std::cout << str << ' ';
    }
    return 0;
}
```
This implementation uses a `std::forward_list<std::string>` to store the strings, and it defines a function `insertAfter` that takes three arguments: the forward list itself, the target string, and the new string. The function searches for the first occurrence of the target string in the forward list using a loop, and then inserts the new string immediately following it. If the target string is not found, the function appends the new string to the end of the list.

To handle performance constraints, this implementation uses a linear search algorithm with a worst-case time complexity of O(n) for an n-element forward list. To improve performance further, the function could be modified to use a binary search algorithm or a hash table-based lookup mechanism.

For scalability, this implementation does not handle cases where the target string appears more than once in the forward list. In such cases, the function should maintain a pointer to the most recent occurrence of the target string and use it as the insertion point for the new string. This can be done by keeping track of the last found occurrence of the target string using a `std::list<std::string>::iterator` variable, which allows us to efficiently update the list as we search for the target string.

To handle integration issues, this implementation assumes that the forward list and target string are both valid inputs. If either of these parameters is invalid (e.g., the target string does not exist in the list), the function should return an error message or throw an exception. To improve robustness, we can also add additional validation checks to ensure that the input parameters meet certain criteria before processing them further.

Overall, this implementation is well-documented and follows best practices for C++ error handling and edge case management. However, it may benefit from more extensive testing and optimization to fully address all potential issues and performance constraints.']

