int modifyFilename(char* filename, char* directoryPath) {
    // Check if the filename and directory path are valid
    if (!filename || !directoryPath) {
        return NULL;
    }

    // Get the original file extension
    char* fileExtension = strrchr(filename, '.');
    if (!fileExtension) {
        return NULL;
    }

    // Determine the number of digits to append to the filename
    int numDigits = 0;
    while (numDigits < 10 && isdigit(fileExtension[numDigits + 1])) {
        numDigits++;
    }

    // Append the new file extension to the filename
    char* modifiedFilename = malloc(strlen(filename) + numDigits + 5);
    strcpy(modifiedFilename, filename);
    sprintf(modifiedFilename + strlen(filename), "_%03d", numDigits);
    strcat(modifiedFilename, fileExtension);

    // Check if the directory path is valid and create it if necessary
    char* dirPath = malloc(strlen(directoryPath) + 1);
    strcpy(dirPath, directoryPath);
    char* lastDirSeparator = strrchr(dirPath, '/');
    if (lastDirSeparator && *(lastDirSeparator + 1) == '\0') {
        *lastDirSeparator = '\0';
    }
    struct stat dirStat;
    int result = stat(dirPath, &dirStat);
    if (result != 0) {
        // Create the directory path if it does not exist
        char* mkdirCommand = malloc(strlen("mkdir -p ") + strlen(dirPath));
        sprintf(mkdirCommand, "mkdir -p %s", dirPath);
        system(mkdirCommand);
        free(mkdirCommand);
    }
    free(dirPath);

    // Return the modified filename and directory path
    char* modifiedFilePath = malloc(strlen(directoryPath) + strlen(modifiedFilename) + 1);
    sprintf(modifiedFilePath, "%s/%s", directoryPath, modifiedFilename);
    free(filename);
    free(modifiedFilename);
    return modifiedFilePath;
}

In this function, we first check if the filename and directory path are valid. If they are not, we return an error code.

Next, we use `strrchr` to get the original file extension from the filename. We then determine the number of digits to append to the filename by analyzing the length of the file extension. We do this by iterating through each character in the file extension and checking if it is a digit using the `isdigit` function.

We then use `sprintf` and `strcat` to construct a new filename with the modified extension and append the new extension to the filename. We also allocate memory for the modified filename and directory path.

After that, we check if the directory path is valid using `stat`. If it is not, we create it using the `mkdir -p` command. We then free the memory used by the `strrchr` function.

Finally, we return the modified filename and directory path as a dynamically allocated string. We also free the memory used by the original filename and directory path.

This function should handle filenames with varying lengths and special characters, ensuring no buffer overflows. It should manage cases where the filename is empty or contains invalid characters, and handle directory paths that may contain nested folders or symbolic links. Implement error handling for situations where the file type cannot be appended due to reserved characters in the original filename or directory path.']

