struct CombatRecord {
  int attackNumber;
};

void manageCombatSequence(int minValue, int maxValue, int maxCooldown) {
  // Initialize an array of 'CombatRecord' structures to hold the attack records
  std::vector<CombatRecord> combatRecords;

  // Set the cooldown period in milliseconds
  const int COOLDOWN_PERIOD = maxCooldown;

  // Generate a random number within the specified range
  std::random_device rd;
  std::mt19937 gen(rd());
  std::uniform_int_distribution<int> distribution(minValue, maxValue);

  while (true) {
    // Generate a new attack number and check if it has cooled down
    int newAttack = distribution(gen);
    std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::time_point now = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();

    bool isCooldownOver = (now - lastAttack) > COOLDOWN_PERIOD;
    if (isCooldownOver) {
      // Add the new attack number to the array of records
      combatRecords.push_back(CombatRecord{newAttack});

      // Print the selected attack number to the console
      std::cout << "Selected attack: " << newAttack << std::endl;

      // Update the last attack time
      lastAttack = now;
    }
  }
}
```
This code will simulate a combat sequence for a multiplayer online battle arena (MOBA) game character. It will initialize an array of `CombatRecord` structures to hold the attack records, and generate a random number within the specified range each time a new attack is selected. The function also implements a cooldown mechanism that ensures that the same attack cannot be used consecutively for 10 seconds before selecting the next attack.

The `manageCombatSequence` function takes three parameters: `minValue`, `maxValue`, and `maxCooldown`. `minValue` and `maxValue` define the range for the random attack numbers, and `maxCooldown` specifies the maximum cooldown duration in milliseconds before the next attack can be selected.

The function uses a `while` loop to simulate the combat sequence continuously until the program is terminated. Inside the loop, it generates a new attack number using a random number generator, checks if the current time has exceeded the cooldown period since the last attack was used, and updates the last attack time if necessary. If the cooldown period has not been exceeded, the function adds the new attack number to the array of records and prints it to the console.

