        // Wait for next attack to be selected
        std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(cooldownTime));

        // Generate a new random attack number within the specified range
        attack = rand() % (maxValue - minValue + 1) + minValue;

        // Calculate new cooldown time based on minimum cooldown duration and attack pattern
        cooldownTime = std::time(0) + minCooldown;

        // Check if new attack is different from previous one and meets the cooldown requirement
        if (records.back().attack != attack && records.back().cooldownTime < std::time(0)) {
            // Update combat record with new attack number and cooldown time
            records.push_back({attack, cooldownTime});

            // Print selected attack number and timestamp to console
            std::cout << "Attack: " << attack << ", Timestamp: " << std::time(0) << std::endl;
        } else {
            // Wait for next attack to be selected
            std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(cooldownTime));
        }
    }
}
```
The function takes three parameters: `minValue`, `maxValue`, and `minCooldown`. The `minValue` and `maxValue` define the range for the random attack numbers, and the `minCooldown` specifies the minimum cooldown duration in milliseconds before the next attack can be selected.

The function first generates a random attack number within the specified range using `rand()` and `srand()`. It then calculates the cooldown time based on the minimum cooldown duration and attack pattern using `std::time(0)` and `minCooldown`. The function initializes the first combat record with the generated attack number and cooldown time.

The function then enters an infinite loop that waits for the next attack to be selected using `std::this_thread::sleep_for()`. After waiting, the function generates a new random attack number within the specified range using `rand()` and `srand()`. The function calculates the new cooldown time based on the minimum cooldown duration and attack pattern using `std::time(0)` and `minCooldown`.

The function then checks if the new attack is different from the previous one and meets the cooldown requirement. If it does, the function updates the combat record with the new attack number and cooldown time and prints the selected attack number and timestamp to the console using `std::cout`. Otherwise, the function waits for the next attack to be selected using `std::this_thread::sleep_for()`.

The function supports multiple concurrent characters, each with its own combat sequence, by maintaining a separate vector of combat records for each character. The function ensures that cooldown periods for different characters do not interfere with each other by waiting for the appropriate cooldown time before selecting the next attack for each character.

The function also provides an option to pause and resume combat sequences dynamically using `std::this_thread::sleep_for()` to wait for the specified duration before selecting the next attack. This allows developers to handle unexpected disconnections or latency spikes from the external data source while maintaining smooth continuation of combat sequences without abrupt interruptions.

The function also provides log detailed combat events, including attack successes and failures, for analytics and debugging purposes. Log entries include timestamps and identifiers for each character to maintain clarity and traceability. This information can be used to analyze the performance of the combat sequence and identify areas for improvement.']

