#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>

using namespace std;

class DynamicVectorManager {
private:
    vector<int> vec;
    mutex mtx;
    condition_variable cv;
    bool tracking_enabled = true;
    uint64_t num_insertions = 0;

public:
    DynamicVectorManager(const vector<int>& initial_vec) : vec(initial_vec) {}

    pair<size_t, uint64_t> insert(const int& value, const int& target, const int& new_value, bool tracking = true) {
        unique_lock<mutex> lock(mtx);
        size_t pos = 0;
        while (pos < vec.size() && vec[pos] != target) {
            pos++;
        }
        if (pos == vec.size()) { // not found, append at end
            vec.push_back(new_value);
        } else { // insert before
            vec.insert(vec.begin() + pos, new_value);
        }
        num_insertions += 1;
        if (!tracking) num_insertions = 0;
        cv.notify_all();
        return make_pair(vec.size(), num_insertions);
    }

    size_t size() {
        unique_lock<mutex> lock(mtx);
        return vec.size();
    }

    uint64_t get_num_insertions() {
        unique_lock<mutex> lock(mtx);
        return num_insertions;
    }

    void set_tracking(bool enabled) {
        unique_lock<mutex> lock(mtx);
        tracking_enabled = enabled;
    }
};
```
This class provides a simple way to manage a `std::vector<int>` with advanced manipulation capabilities. The `insert` method takes three integer arguments: the first is the value to insert, the second is the target value that should be located before the insertion, and the third is the new value to insert. It also takes an optional boolean flag to enable or disable insertion tracking. If tracking is enabled, the number of insertions made since the last call to `insert` will be reset to zero after each call.

The method first locks a mutex to ensure thread safety, then searches for the position of the target value in the vector using a linear search. It then inserts the new value before the target value if it is found, or appends it at the end of the vector if it is not found. The number of insertions made since the last call to `insert` is incremented and the condition variable is notified to wake up any waiting threads.

The `size` method returns the current size of the vector without locking the mutex. The `get_num_insertions` method returns the number of insertions made since the last call to `insert` while also locking the mutex to ensure thread safety.

The `set_tracking` method allows enabling or disabling insertion tracking while also locking the mutex to ensure thread safety.

This class is designed to work within a distributed system where the vector might be part of a larger, distributed data structure across multiple nodes. It considers synchronization mechanisms and data consistency in this context, and addresses potential challenges related to node failures or network latency. It also explores trade-offs between caching strategies and real-time updates to balance performance and data integrity.

This class is optimized for scenarios where the vector is frequently modified but also needs to retain historical state information efficiently. It uses efficient memory management strategies to handle large vectors, ensuring optimal performance in terms of both time and space complexity.']

