In this implementation, we use a mutex to ensure that only one thread can access the dataset at a time. We also use `malloc` and `free` to dynamically manage memory for the arrays of doubles, unsigned longs, data entries, and metadata.

When serializing the dataset, we serialize each array separately, followed by the data entries and metadata. When deserializing the dataset, we first read in the double and unsigned long arrays, then the data entries and metadata. We use `fread` and `fwrite` to read and write the data from and to the file, respectively.

We also provide functions for allocating and deallocating memory dynamically, as well as locking and unlocking the mutex. These functions are used throughout the implementation to ensure that the dataset is properly managed and that concurrent access is safe.

Overall, this implementation addresses the challenges of real-world constraints such as available memory, I/O bandwidth, and processing speed by using a robust memory management system, serialization techniques, and concurrency control mechanisms. It also allows for scalable growth of the dataset, with minimal impact on performance as the number of entries increases.']

