struct CombatRecord {
  int attackNumber;
  std::chrono::time_point<std::chrono::high_resolution_clock> cooldownEndTime;
};

void orchestrateCombatSequence(int minValue, int maxValue, int minCooldown) {
  // Initialize vector of CombatRecord objects
  std::vector<CombatRecord> records;

  // Generate random attack numbers within specified range
  std::random_device rd;
  std::mt19937 gen(rd());
  std::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(minValue, maxValue);

  for (int i = 0; i < records.size(); ++i) {
    records[i].attackNumber = dis(gen);
    records[i].cooldownEndTime = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now() + minCooldown;
  }

  // Loop until cooldown period is reached for all characters
  while (true) {
    bool anyCharacterHasActiveCooldown = false;
    for (int i = 0; i < records.size(); ++i) {
      if (records[i].cooldownEndTime > std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now()) {
        anyCharacterHasActiveCooldown = true;
        break;
      }
    }

    // If no character has an active cooldown, select a new attack for all characters and update their cooldown times
    if (!anyCharacterHasActiveCooldown) {
      for (int i = 0; i < records.size(); ++i) {
        records[i].attackNumber = dis(gen);
        records[i].cooldownEndTime = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now() + minCooldown;
      }
    }

    // Print selected attack numbers and timestamps to console
    for (int i = 0; i < records.size(); ++i) {
      std::cout << "Character " << i + 1 << ": Attack " << records[i].attackNumber << " at " << std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::to_time_t(records[i].cooldownEndTime) << std::endl;
    }
  }
}
```
This implementation uses the `std::random_device` and `std::mt19937` classes to generate random attack numbers within the specified range. It also uses the `std::uniform_int_distribution` class to generate a uniform distribution of attack numbers. The `std::vector` is used to store the `CombatRecord` objects, which contain the attack number and cooldown end time for each character.

The implementation uses a loop that runs until all characters have an active cooldown period. It checks if any character has an active cooldown by checking if its cooldown end time is greater than the current time. If no character has an active cooldown, it selects a new attack for all characters and updates their cooldown times.

The implementation also includes a `std::cout` statement to print the selected attack numbers and timestamps to the console.

Finally, the implementation uses `std::chrono::high_resolution_clock` to get the current time in nanoseconds, which is used to determine when each character's cooldown period ends.']

