3. An external data source for periodic updates, with strategies to handle transient failures gracefully.
4. Data compression to balance memory usage and access speed.
5. Archiving infrequently accessed records to a separate storage medium, reducing the main memory footprint.
6. Efficient data retrieval strategies to quickly restore archived records when needed.
7. Optimization for cache-friendliness and performance-critical operations, prioritizing data structures and algorithms that minimize cache misses and maximize parallelism.
8. Robust error handling and logging to monitor system health and performance, facilitating quick identification and resolution of issues.
9. Unit tests for all functions, covering various input scenarios, including edge cases like zero or negative record counts.
10. Integration tests to verify seamless interaction with the external data source and logging service, ensuring the system behaves predictably under diverse operating conditions.
11. Strict performance constraints, limiting any single update operation to no more than 5 seconds, to prevent bottlenecks and maintain system responsiveness.']

